    "content": "Your framing is the right one. I'd split the benefit into three parts, because they have very different answers.\n\n**Mean latency: usually invisible.** The wire and switch time is the same in both cases, so the difference is host-side. Rough numbers: a well-tuned kernel TCP round trip is ~10-30 µs, with busy polling or io_uring maybe ~8-12 µs, versus ~2-3 µs for RDMA. That 10-20 µs only shows up end to end if it's a meaningful fraction of the operation's critical path. Any request that does a flash read, parses a protobuf, takes a lock, or runs 100 µs of business logic buries it. It matters when the work is a chain of *dependent* small round trips (distributed lock acquisition, pointer-chasing reads in a remote index, transaction commit protocols, in-datacenter consensus on the critical path) and per-hop compute is near zero. There the savings multiply by the depth of the chain. Typical microservice RPC is nowhere near that regime.\n\n**CPU cost per bit: the stronger argument.** At 200-400 Gbps, kernel TCP burns many cores on copies, softirq, and protocol processing, and receive-side memcpy becomes a bandwidth problem. Zero-copy receive, GRO/TSO, and busy polling help a lot, but RDMA still wins on cores per Gbps, and one-sided operations take the remote CPU out of the path entirely. For GPU workloads, GPUDirect RDMA avoids staging through host memory, which TCP can't match. So the real justification is less \"microseconds saved\" and more \"throughput per core\" and \"host memory bandwidth left for the actual work.\"\n\n**Tail latency: cuts both ways.** RDMA removes a lot of host-side jitter (scheduler wakeups, interrupt coalescing, softirq contention). But in the fabric it replaces ordinary drops-and-retransmits with pause propagation, and a bad PFC episode produces a much worse tail than TCP's RTO ever would. Your list of failure modes is right. PFC deadlock needs a cyclic buffer dependency. A clean Clos with strict up-down routing is loop-free, but link failures and rerouting can create transient paths that violate that. Pause storms from a misbehaving NIC are the other classic one, which is why PFC watchdogs are effectively mandatory. And DCQCN tuning is sensitive to topology, flow mix, and buffer configuration in ways that don't transfer between clusters.\n\n**The industry's direction supports your skepticism.** The failure mode is mostly about NIC transport design, since older RoCE NICs use go-back-N and therefore need near-zero loss. The trend among large operators is to keep RDMA semantics while dropping the lossless-fabric requirement: AWS's SRD uses multipath with its own congestion control on a lossy network, Google's Falcon is a similar idea, and the Ultra Ethernet work leans on packet spraying plus selective retransmit. Newer RoCE NICs with selective repeat can also run with ECN and little or no PFC, at some performance cost. So \"RoCE with a full PFC fabric\" is increasingly the legacy answer rather than the default.\n\n**Where I'd say it's justified:**\n- Tightly coupled training or HPC collectives, where one straggler stalls everything, traffic is bulk and regular, and you can dedicate a fabric or at least isolate it.\n- Disaggregated storage (NVMe-oF) and in-memory stores that use one-sided reads, where CPU savings and microsecond reads are the product.\n- Small, dedicated clusters where you control the whole topology and can keep the PFC domain tiny.\n\n**Where I'd stay with TCP:** general-purpose multi-tenant fabrics, anything where service times are 50 µs or more, and anywhere the operators' existing debugging toolchain (tcpdump, ss, familiar queueing behavior) is worth more than the last 10 µs. Before leaving TCP, there's a middle tier worth exhausting: busy polling, io_uring zero-copy receive, pacing/BBR-style or delay-based congestion control, and kernel-bypass TCP stacks like Onload or DPDK-based ones. These recover much of the latency gap with failure modes your operators already understand.\n\nA practical test: measure what fraction of your p50 and p99 request time is spent in the network stack, and separately what fraction of your cores the stack consumes at target load. If the first is under ~10-20% and the second is small, RDMA's gain is rounding error against its operational cost. If either is large, and the traffic is regular enough that you can bound the PFC domain, it's worth a pilot, but I'd start with a lossy or ECN-only configuration on modern NICs before committing to a full lossless build-out. 这是对第七个主题的回复"
