    "content": "Agreed, with two refinements: what's lost is *granularity and observability* more than control, and the boundary is better defined by trust than by tenant count and hops.\n\n**The lever moves to a coarser level.** The server doesn't lose all control, because QP establishment still goes through a two-sided handshake (CM), and it decides what to register and with which access flags. Type 2 memory windows bound to a QP can be revoked cheaply, and deregistering an MR or tearing down a QP is the kill switch. But that's connection- and region-granularity enforcement. You can revoke, but you can't reject one read or defer it behind higher-priority work. The server's policy layer shrinks from \"per request, arbitrary logic\" to \"grant or revoke an entire capability.\"\n\n**Observability goes with it.** Under TCP, a greedy reader shows up in the server's own accounting (CPU, socket queues, per-connection stats), so the server can attribute the load and throttle it. With one-sided reads, the cost lands in NIC, PCIe, and memory-controller resources that no server software sees. Attribution depends on per-QP or per-VF hardware counters, if the NIC exposes them, and on rate limiters that are enforced at the NIC. Those limiters are usually configured by whoever controls the client side or the control plane, which is a different trust relationship from \"the server decides.\"\n\n**The shared resource isn't only memory bandwidth.** NIC microarchitecture is a contention surface too. QP state and MTT/MPT translation entries live in a small on-NIC cache, so a client with many QPs or scattered access across many registered regions can degrade everyone else's throughput without saturating anything obvious. The isolation research (Justitia and Husky at NSDI, if I'm remembering the venues correctly) shows this is a real noisy-neighbor channel. Security analyses like ReDMArk add that rkeys are weak authentication, since holding one is effectively authority, which pushes you toward trusted clients regardless.\n\n**Two caveats on the TCP comparison.** First, TCP's server-side lever protects the server, not the fabric: a greedy reader under TCP still causes incast at the server's downlink, but TCP's loss-based or delay-based feedback bounds that damage, whereas pause-based fabrics propagate it. Second, in some deployments the target has no policy worth protecting. A disaggregated NVMe-oF target or a memory pool is deliberately dumb, so admission control already lives at the initiator or a control plane, and the lost lever costs you little.\n\n**Hybrids that recover part of the lever.** Two-sided RDMA (SEND/RECV RPC) keeps server admission control and still saves a lot of CPU versus kernel TCP. One-sided reads can be reserved for a hot path with server-issued, short-lived capabilities (leases on windows), so the server periodically re-decides who may read. That puts back a coarse form of control.\n\nOn your framing, I'd refine the design variable to a **trust and change domain**: who can issue one-sided operations against whom, and who shares a PFC domain, firmware version, and config-push pipeline. Tenant and hop counts are proxies for that. A single team's homogeneous training pod or storage tier is a domain where a greedy client is a bug you can fix. A multi-tenant fabric isn't. So RDMA inside a bounded, homogeneous domain, with TCP (or a lossy RDMA-like transport such as SRD) at the boundaries, is the shape that falls out. Note that SRD and Falcon address the fabric half of your concern, not the greedy-reader half, which remains a policy problem on the endpoint side."
