    "content": "Folding the two refinements into one is right: without a view of the in-flight set, no revocation is timely. But the first factor is less outside the target's world than you've put it, and that changes the pricing.\n\n**The queue-depth factor is partly a grant-time parameter.** Validation happens at the responder when a request arrives, not when the client posts it. After you invalidate a window or move the QP to error, anything still sitting in the client's send queue hits a remote access NAK. What completes is only what has already crossed validation, and for reads that is bounded by responder resources, which the target sets in the CM handshake (`max_dest_rd_atomic`), times the read length, which the window size caps. So exposure is roughly (QPs × responder depth × op size) drained at NIC read-pipeline rate, plus time-to-notice. The target can't see the client's posted depth, but it can cap the part that matters at admission, and it can budget that product per client class. That makes grant-time admission a real lever, though a static one. Writes and sends are bounded differently, so this is a statement about reads. Also, the invalidation itself isn't free or instantaneous: dereg or window invalidation has its own latency and may wait on outstanding ops, so I'd measure it rather than assume it.\n\n**Continuous rate limiting is the weak leg.** The enforcement point has to be hardware, and the granularity you get is typically per VF or per traffic class on the target's NIC, not per QP or per client. Read responses are target-generated egress traffic, and I wouldn't assume per-QP requester-side limiters cover them, so check what your NIC actually shapes. In practice this pushes you to give each client class its own VF or QoS class on the target, which is coarse but enforceable. It also needs the counters from the observability leg to tell you when to tighten.\n\n**Leases solve a different threat than the one we're discussing.** A lease bounds the lifetime of a grant. It doesn't bound rate within the grant or detect a greedy client: a client at line rate for the whole TTL is fully compliant. What leases buy is fail-safe expiry for clients the target can't reach or doesn't know about: crashed, partitioned, or zombie holders, and stale rkeys surviving a memory reassignment. That's a real problem, and arguably the more common one operationally, but it's staleness, not greed. For greed, non-renewal is just revocation by another name, and it still depends on the target having noticed.\n\n**Pricing the lease.** Let R be the longest control-plane outage you need the fast path to survive, and E the longest exposure window you'll tolerate for a client you've decided to cut off. If clients renew at TTL/2, the fast path survives outages up to TTL/2, so you need TTL ≥ 2R and TTL ≤ E, which is only feasible when E ≥ 2R. Two other costs:\n- Renewal load is clients × windows / TTL, and it lands on the target's CPU, the resource one-sided access was supposed to spare. It's off the data path, but it's still a two-sided protocol that has to stay responsive.\n- If the renewal path is a host-side agent, you've re-coupled the fast path to host health. Moving it to a DPU or NIC firmware removes that coupling but puts policy in a new, less debuggable bug surface, which is your original objection again.\n\nIf E < 2R, leases can't work, and what's left is the hardware-enforced coarse option: per-class VFs with rate limits, short admission-time budgets, and an explicit decision that the domain only contains clients you can fix.\n\n**Budgeted leases.** One variant puts a byte budget in the grant alongside the TTL, so renewal also acts as a quota refill. The target can't take the client's word for consumption, so it needs the NIC counters again, but renewal gives it a natural point to act on them. It's also a cheap way to get something like continuous control from a mechanism that is periodic by construction.\n\nThe question to answer before choosing the hybrid is therefore the pair (R, E) for your domain, plus whether your NICs expose per-VF or per-QP counters at useful granularity. If E ≥ 2R and the counters exist, budgeted leases are defensible. If not, the isolation boundary has to shrink until a greedy client is a bug you can fix, rather than a policy problem you have to enforce."
