    "content": "A few of the premises need adjusting, and the adjustments change what an effect system would have to do.\n\n**Cancellation isn't a soundness hole, and Rust does have affine types.** Moves give you affine semantics, and `Drop` runs on every cancelled path, so the language already guarantees that resources owned by the future get released. What it can't express is the logical invariant: \"if I'm dropped between these two awaits, the world I touched is still consistent.\" That's a correctness problem, not a safety one, which is part of why it stays invisible until load makes timeouts and `select!` branches fire. What's missing is linear types (must-complete) and async `Drop`, not affinity. `Drop` is synchronous, so cleanup that needs I/O can't run on cancellation.\n\n**Pinning is mostly orthogonal.** Drop glue handles partially initialized state field by field, and `Pin`'s drop guarantee exists to make intrusive and self-referential structures sound. It's why a cancelled future can't leave dangling pointers. The pinning pain shows up elsewhere, in ergonomics and pin projection.\n\n**Where annotations would go.** Every `.await` is already a syntactically visible cancel point, so an effect marking \"can be cancelled here\" adds almost nothing. The property you actually care about is different: does the future leave externally visible partial effects across a suspension? That isn't about control flow, it's about state:\n\n- A future that owns all its state is trivially cancel-safe, because dropping it just discards the state.\n- Trouble starts when it mutates things that outlive it: `&mut` borrows, `Arc<Mutex<_>>`, or the stream itself. `read_exact` is the classic case, where bytes consumed from a socket are lost on cancellation. Plain `read` is safe.\n- So the property is closer to atomicity or transactionality of a region, and tokio's per-method \"cancel safety\" doc sections are an informal version of it.\n\n**Would a \"cancel-unsafe\" effect be practical?** Technically it could propagate like `unsafe` or `async`, with `select!` and `timeout` demanding cancel-safe arguments. The costs are steep:\n\n- Polymorphism: generic code over `F: Future` needs effect-polymorphism. Keyword generics struggled to make that work for `async` and `const`, and this would be a third axis.\n- Trait objects and dyn futures erase the information.\n- It's not compositional. Whether `a().await; b().await` is safe depends on whether the state `a` touched is observable, which a function-level annotation can't see. You'd be annotating with the conservative answer everywhere, and a pervasive conservative annotation gets ignored.\n- Existing ecosystem code would need retrofitting.\n\n**More promising partial approaches:**\n\n- Async `Drop` (experimental) closes the \"cleanup needs I/O\" gap.\n- A lint or marker at the cancellation boundary. `select!` and `timeout` are where cancellation is introduced, so a narrow \"this future was documented cancel-safe\" check there is cheap and avoids infecting everything else.\n- Drop-bomb or guard types that make partial states explicit, such as a guard that rolls back, or one that panics in debug builds if dropped mid-transaction. This is typestate-flavored and works today.\n- Design patterns that avoid the problem: spawn the critical section so it runs to completion regardless of the caller, or make a single task own the resource and communicate over channels. Cancelling the requester then only drops a message.\n- Cooperative cancellation (Kotlin, Swift, Trio) makes cancel points explicit and checkable, but gives up Rust's zero-cost \"drop is cancel\" model, so it's a different language design.\n\nRain's writing on cancellation at Oxide, including the \"futurelock\" work, is worth reading if you haven't, since it catalogs these failure modes from real production incidents.\n\nSo my read is that it's both a gap and tribal knowledge. The knowledge is real design (own your state, spawn critical sections, keep `select!` branches cancel-safe), but it exists because the language lacks a way to state the atomicity contract. A function-level effect probably isn't the right shape, since the contract attaches to regions and state, not functions. Async drop plus guard types plus a boundary-level check seems like the realistic ceiling. Do you see a shape for the annotation that escapes the \"it depends on what state is observable\" problem, or are you leaning toward a region-based approach?"
