    "content": "The crossover isn't really a function of request rate and lifetime. It splits into two separate boundaries that depend on different combinations of them.\n\n## Two boundaries\n\nJIT warmup is driven by invocation counts, not wall-clock time. Per instance, with rate λ and lifetime L:\n\n- **Throughput boundary** depends on N = λL, the total requests an instance serves.\n- **Latency boundary** depends on T_w = n_w/λ, the wall-clock duration of the cliff, which *shrinks* as per-instance rate rises.\n\n**Throughput.** Let c_j be steady-state JIT cost per request, r = c_a/c_j the steady-state AOT penalty, m the cold-start cost multiplier relative to c_j, and n₀ the warmup scale in requests. Excess cost during warmup is roughly c_j(m−1)n₀, plus compile CPU and any startup delta. Savings after warmup are c_j(r−1) per request. So:\n\nN* ≈ n₀(m−1)/(r−1) + (compile CPU + startup delta)/(c_j(r−1))\n\nWith m=20, n₀=20k, r=1.3, that's about 1.3M requests. At 1k rps per instance that's ~20 minutes. At 50 rps it's ~7 hours, so a fleet that autoscales with hour-scale lifetimes never repays the warmup. N* scales inversely with (r−1), so the answer is extremely sensitive to r, which is the number people usually hand-wave.\n\n**Latency.** With fleet scale-out/churn fraction g per unit time, the cold share of traffic is about g·T_w. Your p99 breaks when that share exceeds (1−q). That gives a floor on per-instance rate, λ > n_w·g/(1−q), a horizontal line on a (L, λ) plane. The throughput boundary is a hyperbola λL = N*. JIT wins only above the line and right of the hyperbola. Low-rate-per-instance services (many small replicas) lose on both axes, which is why the cliff feels worst exactly when you scale out.\n\n## The cliff is mostly an interpreter-floor artifact\n\nThe multiplier m = 20 comes from falling back to an interpreter. If the floor is AOT code instead (ReadyToRun-then-TieredPGO, ART's profile-guided AOT, V8's baseline tiers), then m collapses toward r. Warmup cost becomes c_j(r−1)n₀ and the crossover largely disappears: you start at AOT speed and only gain from there. What remains is compile CPU, code-cache memory, and deopt storms when speculation fails. The real design question becomes whether you can afford the tier-up machinery, not JIT vs AOT. Caveat: AOT floor code is often worse than plain AOT because of indirections and no cross-module inlining, so measure it.\n\n## r is not a constant\n\nDecompose it:\n\n- **Closed-world devirtualization** (CHA, type-flow analysis) recovers much of the dynamic-dispatch gap when reflection is limited. Penalty is large mainly for open-world code.\n- **Offline PGO** recovers a good share of inline-cache-style gains, if the training profile is representative. Commonly reported results put it well above half the gap, but treat any specific number as workload-dependent.\n- **What only a JIT gets** is speculation with deopt guards, phase adaptation, and megamorphic sites that change over the process lifetime. This is the residual r that justifies JIT, and it's driven by workload non-stationarity. A stationary request mix gives r near 1.1; a drifting one can give 1.5+.\n\nSo estimate r on held-out traffic from a different phase than your PGO training trace, not on the training trace itself.\n\n## WebAssembly\n\nBoundary cost per request is roughly k·(c_call + b·c_copy), where k is crossings and b is bytes per crossing. Isolation constrains these differently:\n\n- **c_call** is inlinable. Separate memories don't forbid a whole-program compiler from inlining the adapter trampoline.\n- **c_copy** is not elidable. The canonical ABI's lift/lower copies and callee-side allocation are semantically required when memories are separate. This dominates for string/list-heavy interfaces.\n\nSo the boundary penalty is roughly symmetric between JIT and AOT. It doesn't move the crossover much; it changes the absolute floor. The granularity rule is to put boundaries where bytes crossing are small relative to compute behind them.\n\nWhere Wasm does shift the picture:\n\n- Instantiation is microseconds with pooling allocators and copy-on-write memory init, so with precompiled modules cold start is nearly free. AOT wins cold start decisively.\n- For Rust/C++ guests, PGO happened before Wasm, and the code is already statically typed. r ≈ 1.0–1.1, so JIT has little to offer.\n- The ugly case is dynamic languages in Wasm: most server hosts can't JIT guest code, so you get an interpreter running in Wasm with neither JIT nor ordinary AOT. Snapshotting (Wizer-style) and partial evaluation of the interpreter against its bytecode are the levers there. The latter is promising but newer, so verify current results before relying on it.\n\n## What to measure\n\n1. Replay production traffic into fresh instances and plot latency against request index, not time. That gives you m and n₀ directly.\n2. Compile-thread CPU during warmup, as a fraction of cores. It's usually missing from \"JIT is slower at startup\" arguments and matters when scaling out under load.\n3. Steady-state JIT vs AOT+PGO on held-out traffic for r.\n4. Boundary microbenchmarks with realistic payload sizes for k and b.\n\nThen N* and the latency line fall out, and you can see which mitigations (snapshots, shared code caches, AOT floors) move which boundary. Want me to draw the (L, λ) plane with those regions and show how each mitigation shifts them?"
